2026-02-10 22:36:22,481 - DocumentGraderNode - INFO: Retrieved document Options

An options contract is similar to a futures contract in that it's an agreement between two parties to buy or sell an asset at a predetermined future date for a specific price. Unlike futures, an options buyer isn't obliged to exercise their agreement to buy or sell. It's an opportunity only, not an obligation, as futures are. As with futures, options may be used to hedge or speculate on the price of the underlying asset. is relevant
2026-02-10 22:36:23,524 - _client - INFO: HTTP Request: POST https://dashscope.aliyuncs.com/compatible-mode/v1/chat/completions "HTTP/1.1 200 OK"
2026-02-10 22:36:23,527 - DocumentGraderNode - INFO: Retrieved document Exploring the Types of Options: Calls and Puts is relevant
2026-02-10 22:36:24,460 - _client - INFO: HTTP Request: POST https://dashscope.aliyuncs.com/compatible-mode/v1/chat/completions "HTTP/1.1 200 OK"
2026-02-10 22:36:24,464 - DocumentGraderNode - INFO: Retrieved document Forex Market vs. Other Markets

There are some fundamental differences between foreign exchange and other markets.

First of all, there are fewer rules, which means investors aren't held to strict standards or regulations like those in the stock, futures, and options markets. There are no clearing houses and no central bodies that oversee the forex market.

Second, since trades don't take place on a traditional exchange, there are fewer fees or commissions like those on other markets. Next, there's no cutoff as to when you can and cannot trade. Because the market is open 24 hours a day, you can trade at any time.

Finally, because it's such a liquid market, you can get in and out whenever you want and you can buy as much currency as you can afford. is NOT relevant, dump it.
