2026-02-10 22:36:25,842 - _client - INFO: HTTP Request: POST https://dashscope.aliyuncs.com/compatible-mode/v1/chat/completions "HTTP/1.1 200 OK"
2026-02-10 22:36:25,846 - DocumentGraderNode - INFO: Retrieved document Understanding the Mechanics of Warrants

While warrants and options are similar, they have key differences. Companies typically issue warrants, which are often traded over-the-counter rather than on exchanges. Investors cannot write warrants like they can options.

Unlike options, warrants are dilutive. When investors exercise a warrant, they get newly issued stock instead of existing shares. Warrants tend to have much longer periods between issue and expiration than options, of years rather than months.

Warrants do not pay dividends or come with voting rights. Investors like warrants for leverage, hedging against risk, or taking advantage of arbitrage opportunities. is relevant
