A futures contract buyer must take possession of the underlying stocks or shares at the time of expiration—not before. Buyers may sell their positions before expiration. There is a difference between options and futures. American-style options give the holder the right, but not the obligation, to buy or sell the underlying asset anytime before the expiration date of the contract. is relevant
